{%- comment -%}
  Collection — ported from CollectionPage.jsx. Banner, then the category rail +
  filter panel beside the product grid, then the category SEO block.

  Differences forced by the platform, all deliberate:
    · Sorting is Shopify's (sort_by), not a client-side comparator. The option
      labels are the design's own; "Top rated" maps to best-selling because
      Shopify cannot sort by rating.
    · The grid paginates. The React page rendered every product at once, which
      is fine for 132 local objects and not fine for a live storefront.
    · The SEO blocks come from the collection description rather than a
      hand-authored block list.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign per_page = section.settings.products_per_page -%}

<main id="main" class="col">
  {%- comment -%}
    BANNER — a real <img>, not a CSS background.

    A background-image is the slowest possible way to load the largest thing on
    the page: the browser can't see it until the stylesheet has downloaded,
    parsed and matched, so it starts late, downloads at low priority, and can't
    be given a srcset — every phone was pulling the same 2000px file.

    As an <img> it is discovered in the initial HTML scan, marked
    fetchpriority="high", and sized per device. No compression is added beyond
    Shopify's own CDN encoding; the widths below only stop a 390px phone
    downloading a desktop-sized file.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    BANNER SOURCE — three tiers, most specific first.

    `custom.banner` exists because `collection.image` cannot serve two masters:
    it is also the card art in mts-list-collections, mts-category-showcase,
    mts-industries-index and mts-industries, where a 5:1 strip cropped to a
    120px square shows a sliver of its own middle. The metafield holds the
    art-directed 2000x400 banner; the collection image stays the card.

    A collection with no metafield keeps falling back to its image, so this is
    additive — nothing is blanked by introducing it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign banner = blank
    assign banner_mf = collection.metafields.custom.banner.value
    if section.settings.banner_image != blank
      assign banner = section.settings.banner_image
    elsif banner_mf != blank
      assign banner = banner_mf
    elsif collection.image
      assign banner = collection.image
    elsif settings.default_collection_banner != blank
      assign banner = settings.default_collection_banner
    endif
  -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The default sits LAST on purpose. It is the catch-all for a collection with
    no art of its own — most importantly /collections/all, which is Shopify's
    virtual all-products route: it has no collection record at all, so it can
    carry neither an image nor a metafield, and was the one collection page
    rendering with a bare header. Any collection that does have its own banner
    is unaffected, because this branch is only reached when the other three
    produce nothing.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The tiny inline background is a low-quality placeholder. `.colban` is
    charcoal, so until the real banner decodes the whole band paints solid
    black — which reads as a broken page rather than a loading one. A 24px-wide
    copy of the SAME image arrives in a few hundred bytes and, blown up to full
    width, gives an instantly-correct colour wash that the sharp image then
    resolves into.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="colban grain{% if banner != blank %} colban--img{% endif %}"
       {% if banner != blank %}style="background-image:url({{ banner | image_url: width: 24 }})"{% endif %}>
    {%- if banner != blank -%}
      <img class="colban__img"
           src="{{ banner | image_url: width: 1600 }}"
           srcset="{{ banner | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w,
                   {{ banner | image_url: width: 1200 }} 1200w,
                   {{ banner | image_url: width: 1600 }} 1600w,
                   {{ banner | image_url: width: 2000 }} 2000w"
           sizes="100vw"
           alt=""
           aria-hidden="true"
           {%- comment -%}
             The intrinsic ratio must describe the ART, not the band. These said
             2000x560 (3.57:1) while the banners are 2000x400 (5:1), so any
             browser inferring an aspect-ratio from the attributes got a shape
             the file never had.

             This cannot move the band: `.colban__img` is
             position:absolute; inset:0; width:100%; height:100% — the band's
             height comes from `.colban__inner`'s padding and its text, so the
             attributes are advisory only and the height is untouched.
           {%- endcomment -%}
           width="2000" height="400"
           fetchpriority="high"
           decoding="async">
    {%- endif -%}
    <div class="colban__scrim"></div>
    <div class="wrap colban__inner">
      {% render 'mts-breadcrumbs', parent_title: section.settings.parent_label, parent_url: section.settings.parent_url %}
      {%- if section.settings.parent_label != blank -%}
        <span class="eyebrow eyebrow--onink">{{ section.settings.parent_label }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Do NOT write `collection.title | default: 'key' | t` — Liquid applies `t`
        to whatever `default` returns, so a real title like "Butyl Tape" gets
        translated and renders as "Translation missing: en.Butyl Tape".
        Translate only the fallback.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <h1 class="colban__title">
        {%- if collection.title != blank -%}
          {{ collection.title }}
        {%- else -%}
          {{ 'collections.general.all_products' | t }}
        {%- endif -%}
      </h1>
      <span class="colban__count num">
        {{ 'collections.general.available_count' | t: count: collection.products_count }}
      </span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap col__layout">
    {%- comment -%}
      On a phone the category rail and the filter groups stacked open above the
      grid and pushed the first product to ~1,136px — well past an 844px
      viewport, so the page opened on controls instead of stock. This toolbar
      collapses both into one box that is closed by default; the aside itself is
      unchanged for desktop, where there is a column to put it in.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="col__mobilebar" data-mts-colbar>
      <button type="button" class="col__mobilebar-btn" data-mts-colbar-toggle="cats" aria-expanded="false">
        {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'grid', size: 16 %}
        <span>Category</span>
        {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'chevronDown', size: 15, class: 'col__mobilebar-chev' %}
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="col__mobilebar-btn" data-mts-colbar-toggle="filters" aria-expanded="false">
        {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'layers', size: 16 %}
        <span>Filters</span>
        {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'chevronDown', size: 15, class: 'col__mobilebar-chev' %}
      </button>
    </div>

    <aside class="col__side" data-mts-colside>
      {%- if section.settings.rail_menu != blank -%}
        {% render 'mts-category-rail', menu_handle: section.settings.rail_menu, active_handle: collection.handle %}
      {%- endif -%}
      {% render 'mts-filter-panel' %}
    </aside>

    <div class="col__main">
      <div class="col__toolbar">
        <span class="col__count num">{{ 'collections.general.products_count' | t: count: collection.products_count }}</span>
        <div class="col__tools">
          <form class="col__sort-form" data-mts-sort>
            {%- comment -%} preserve any active filters when the sort changes {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- for filter in collection.filters -%}
              {%- for value in filter.active_values -%}
                <input type="hidden" name="{{ value.param_name }}" value="{{ value.value }}">
              {%- endfor -%}
              {%- if filter.type == 'price_range' -%}
                {%- if filter.min_value.value -%}
                  <input type="hidden" name="{{ filter.min_value.param_name }}" value="{{ filter.min_value.value | money_without_currency | replace: ',', '' }}">
                {%- endif -%}
                {%- if filter.max_value.value -%}
                  <input type="hidden" name="{{ filter.max_value.param_name }}" value="{{ filter.max_value.value | money_without_currency | replace: ',', '' }}">
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
            <label class="col__sort">
              <span>{{ 'collections.general.sort_by' | t }}</span>
              {%- assign sort_by = collection.sort_by | default: collection.default_sort_by -%}
              <select name="sort_by" aria-label="Sort products">
                <option value="manual" {% if sort_by == 'manual' %}selected{% endif %}>{{ 'collections.sorting.featured' | t }}</option>
                <option value="price-ascending" {% if sort_by == 'price-ascending' %}selected{% endif %}>{{ 'collections.sorting.price_ascending' | t }}</option>
                <option value="price-descending" {% if sort_by == 'price-descending' %}selected{% endif %}>{{ 'collections.sorting.price_descending' | t }}</option>
                <option value="best-selling" {% if sort_by == 'best-selling' %}selected{% endif %}>{{ 'collections.sorting.best_selling' | t }}</option>
                <option value="title-ascending" {% if sort_by == 'title-ascending' %}selected{% endif %}>{{ 'collections.sorting.az' | t }}</option>
              </select>
              {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'chevronDown', size: 16 %}
            </label>
            <noscript><button type="submit" class="btn btn--ghost">Sort</button></noscript>
          </form>
        </div>
      </div>

      {%- paginate collection.products by per_page -%}
        {%- if collection.products.size > 0 -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            PINNED ORDER

            These categories are smart collections, so Shopify offers no manual
            sort — but the old site put its bestsellers on the first row, and
            returning customers navigate by that shape. `custom.product_order`
            carries it: the listed products render first, in order, and anything
            else follows in Shopify's own order.

            Two rules keep it honest rather than a snapshot that rots:
              · a pinned product renders only if it is still IN this collection
                (checked against `member_keys`), so losing its tag removes it
              · pinning is skipped once the grid paginates or the shopper picks
                a sort — reordering page 1 of 3 would just scramble the set

            Handles are compared pipe-delimited so `butyl-tape` cannot match
            inside `butyl-tape-black`.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign pinned = collection.metafields.custom.product_order.value

            # `collection.sort_by` is set ONLY by ?sort_by= in the URL, so a
            # blank value means the shopper has not chosen a sort and the pinned
            # order should win. Do NOT fall back to `default_sort_by` here: that
            # is the admin's default (best-selling on every category in this
            # store), which would read as an explicit choice and disable pinning
            # on every page.
            # `manual` is what the sort dropdown's "Featured" option submits, and
            # featured IS the pinned order — so it keeps pinning on rather than
            # turning it off. Any other value is a real shopper choice (price,
            # A–Z, best-selling) and Shopify's ordering must win.
            assign shopper_sorted = false
            if collection.sort_by != blank and collection.sort_by != 'manual'
              assign shopper_sorted = true
            endif

            assign use_pinned = false
            if pinned != blank and paginate.pages <= 1 and shopper_sorted == false
              assign use_pinned = true
            endif

            if use_pinned
              assign member_keys = '|'
              for p in collection.products
                assign member_keys = member_keys | append: p.handle | append: '|'
              endfor
            endif
          -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            Visually hidden, but it closes the h1 -> h3 gap: the card titles are
            H3 and had no H2 above them. Screen readers and crawlers get a
            complete outline; nothing changes on screen.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <h2 class="sr-only">{{ 'collections.general.products_in' | t: title: collection.title }}</h2>
          <div class="grid-products col__grid">
            {%- if use_pinned -%}
              {%- assign shown = '|' -%}
              {%- for product in pinned -%}
                {%- assign key = '|' | append: product.handle | append: '|' -%}
                {%- if member_keys contains key -%}
                  {%- unless shown contains key -%}
                    {%- assign shown = shown | append: product.handle | append: '|' -%}
                    {% render 'mts-product-card', product: product, index: forloop.index0, hide_cat: true %}
                  {%- endunless -%}
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
              {%- for product in collection.products -%}
                {%- assign key = '|' | append: product.handle | append: '|' -%}
                {%- unless shown contains key -%}
                  {% render 'mts-product-card', product: product, index: forloop.index0, hide_cat: true %}
                {%- endunless -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
            {%- else -%}
              {%- for product in collection.products -%}
                {% render 'mts-product-card', product: product, index: forloop.index0, hide_cat: true %}
              {%- endfor -%}
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>

          {%- if paginate.pages > 1 -%}
            {% render 'mts-pagination', paginate: paginate %}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- else -%}
          <div class="col__empty">
            {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'layers', size: 40 %}
            <p>{{ 'collections.general.no_matches' | t }}</p>
            <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="{{ collection.url }}">{{ 'collections.general.clear_filters' | t }}</a>
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endpaginate -%}
    </div>
  </div>

  {%- liquid
    assign seo_blocks = collection.metafields.custom.seo_blocks.value

    # An "Overview" card whose entire contents is the category name repeats the
    # H1 the reader just passed and frames it as if it were a summary. Two
    # categories are in exactly that state, and they render as a large empty
    # panel with a heading and nothing under it. Compare the stripped text to
    # the title and drop the card when it adds nothing.
    assign intro_text = collection.description | strip_html | strip
    assign show_intro = false
    if intro_text != blank
      assign a = intro_text | downcase | replace: '.', '' | strip
      assign b = collection.title | downcase | strip
      if a != b and intro_text.size > 40
        assign show_intro = true
      endif
    endif

    assign has_body = false
    if show_intro or seo_blocks.size > 0
      assign has_body = true
    endif
  -%}
  {%- if has_body or section.settings.show_seo_close -%}
    <section class="section catseo{% unless has_body %} catseo--bare{% endunless %}">
      <div class="wrap catseo__main">
        {%- if has_body -%}
        <div class="catseo__card">
          {%- if show_intro -%}
            <div class="catseo__intro">
              {%- comment -%}
                No "Overview" eyebrow. It labelled the intro without adding
                anything a reader needs, and it was the first text Shopify found
                when auto-generating the meta description for this page.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {{ collection.description }}
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            The typed blocks, rebuilt with the SAME markup the React CatBlock
            component produced — headings get .catseo__heading, bullets get
            .catseo__list with a check icon each, titled entries get
            .catseo__items, and the FAQ is a real accordion rather than a run of
            headings. Rendering these from JSON (rather than flattened rich text)
            is what makes the page structurally identical to the original.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if seo_blocks -%}
            {%- for block in seo_blocks -%}
              {%- case block.type -%}

                {%- when 'heading' -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    H2: these are the page's own sections, the same role the
                    tab labels play on a product page. Anything nested inside
                    them is H3.

                    A "FAQs:" heading is skipped — the migrated copy introduces
                    the questions with one, and the accordion that follows is
                    self-evident. Matching on the text rather than editing the
                    stored copy means nothing is lost if it is ever wanted back.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    Strip trailing punctuation before comparing — the migrated
                    copy writes this heading as "FAQs:", "FAQs-" and "FAQs."
                    on different categories.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  {%- assign htext = block.text | downcase | strip | replace: ':', '' | replace: '-', '' | replace: '–', '' | replace: '.', '' | strip -%}
                  {%- unless htext == 'faq' or htext == 'faqs' or htext == 'frequently asked questions' -%}
                    <h2 class="catseo__heading">{{ block.text }}</h2>
                  {%- endunless -%}

                {%- when 'p' -%}
                  <p>{{ block.text }}</p>

                {%- when 'list' -%}
                  <ul class="catseo__list">
                    {%- for item in block.items -%}
                      <li>{% render 'mts-icon', name: 'check', size: 14 %}<span>{{ item }}</span></li>
                    {%- endfor -%}
                  </ul>

                {%- when 'items' -%}
                  <ul class="catseo__items">
                    {%- for item in block.items -%}
                      <li>
                        {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'check', size: 15 %}
                        <span>{% if item.title != blank %}<b>{{ item.title }} </b>{% endif %}{{ item.text }}</span>
                      </li>
                    {%- endfor -%}
                  </ul>

                {%- when 'faq' -%}
                  {%- if block.qas.size > 0 -%}
                    {%- comment -%}
                      No heading here. The questions were introduced twice — a
                      "FAQs:" heading block from the migrated copy AND this one —
                      and neither is wanted on a category page. The accordion
                      keeps its eyebrow, which labels it without adding to the
                      document outline.
                    {%- endcomment -%}
                    <div class="catseo__faqs">
                      <h2 class="catseo__faqs-title">FAQs</h2>
                      <ul class="faq__list" data-mts-faq>
                        {%- for qa in block.qas -%}
                          <li class="faq__item{% if forloop.first %} is-open{% endif %}">
                            {%- comment -%}
                              The question is a heading that wraps the control,
                              which is the ARIA accordion pattern: the h3 puts
                              each question in the document outline under the
                              FAQs h2, and the button keeps the expand/collapse
                              semantics. .faq__qh carries no styling of its own.
                            {%- endcomment -%}
                            <h3 class="faq__qh">
                              <button class="faq__q" data-mts-faq-q
                                      aria-expanded="{% if forloop.first %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}">
                                <span>{{ qa.q }}</span>
                                <span data-mts-faq-icon>
                                  {%- if forloop.first -%}
                                    {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'minus', size: 18 %}
                                  {%- else -%}
                                    {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'plus', size: 18 %}
                                  {%- endif -%}
                                </span>
                              </button>
                            </h3>
                            <div class="faq__a" data-mts-faq-a {% unless forloop.first %}hidden{% endunless %}>
                              <p>{{ qa.a }}</p>
                            </div>
                          </li>
                        {%- endfor -%}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  {%- endif -%}
              {%- endcase -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          The CTA sits OUTSIDE the copy card now. It used to be the last child
          of it, so on a category with no copy the only way to show the CTA was
          to render an empty card around it — which is what produced a large
          blank panel with a heading and nothing beneath. Out here it stands on
          its own and needs no container.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if section.settings.show_seo_close -%}
          <div class="catseo__close">
            <p>{{ section.settings.close_text }}</p>
            <a href="{{ section.settings.close_url }}" class="btn btn--brand catseo__contact">
              {{ section.settings.close_cta }} {% render 'mts-icon', name: 'arrowRight', size: 16 %}
            </a>
          </div>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    </section>
  {%- endif -%}
</main>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Collection",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "link_list", "id": "rail_menu", "label": "\"Shop by category\" rail menu", "info": "A nested menu: each top-level link is a group heading, its children are the categories." },
    { "type": "range", "id": "products_per_page", "label": "Products per page", "min": 12, "max": 60, "step": 4, "default": 48 },
    { "type": "image_picker", "id": "banner_image", "label": "Banner image override", "info": "Applies to every collection. Per collection, set the Banner metafield instead; failing both, the collection's own image is used." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "parent_label", "label": "Banner eyebrow / breadcrumb parent", "info": "e.g. \"Single-Sided Tapes\"." },
    { "type": "url", "id": "parent_url", "label": "Breadcrumb parent link" },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Category copy footer" },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_seo_close", "label": "Show the \"talk to our experts\" close", "default": true },
    { "type": "text", "id": "close_text", "label": "Close text", "default": "Still not sure which tape is right for the job?" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "close_cta", "label": "Close button", "default": "Talk to our tape experts" },
    { "type": "url", "id": "close_url", "label": "Close button link" }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
